{
 "coreName": "Xenia",
 "systemId": "X360",
 "systemNames": {
  "X360": "Xbox 360"
 },
 "author": "The Xenia project and the Xenia Canary contributors; chimera port by Sergio Martin",
 "url": "https://github.com/ToolAssisted-run/chimera-core-xenia",
 "romFile": "game",
 "deterministic": true,
 "memoryLayoutMiB": [
  4096,
  16,
  64,
  64,
  20480
 ],
 "_memoryLayoutMiB_note": "sbrk, sealed, invisible, plain, mmap. The console's 4 GiB address space is one reservation on the mmap heap, which must begin on a multiple of 4 GiB with the recompiler's table after it (12.5 GiB are reserved to find one; only the pages a game touches cost anything). The heap is the emulator's own: the recompiled code, the renderer's caches, and what the graphics device held when a state was saved.",
 "video": {
  "_comment": "The picture the game shows, drawn by Xenia's Vulkan renderer through the Vulkan bridge and read back when a frame ends, at the size the game draws it. The console tells games the television is 1280x720 (virtualWidth and virtualHeight: what the picture is shown as), but some draw their picture at 1920x1080 and let the console scale it (DuckTales Remastered, issue 270), so the buffer is 1920x1080, the largest picture an Xbox 360 sends. 60 Hz exactly: the vertical blank is raised every 1/60 s of the machine's time, counted from when it was switched on. With the renderer that draws nothing the picture is black.",
  "width": 1920,
  "height": 1080,
  "virtualWidth": 1280,
  "virtualHeight": 720,
  "vsyncNumerator": 60,
  "vsyncDenominator": 1,
  "getBgra": "GetVideoBgra",
  "gpuStatesSurviveTheContext": true,
  "_comment_gpuStates": "A load is a new graphics device. The renderer is made again on it, and what only the old device held - the console's render targets, and what it resolved into memory - was fetched into the machine before the state was saved (StateSaving) and is put back. The gate's legs are the proof: a state saved and loaded before every frame, and one carried into another process, end with the pictures of a run that was never stopped."
 },
 "audio": {
  "_comment": "What the program hands the console's sound output - 256 samples of six channels at 48 kHz - mixed down to stereo and taken by the frame: about 800 pairs a frame, in steps of 256.",
  "rate": 48000,
  "samplesPerFrame": 4096,
  "channels": 2,
  "get": "GetAudio"
 },
 "lag": {
  "inputWasRead": "InputWasRead"
 },
 "input": {
  "name": "Xbox 360 Controllers",
  "_comment": "Four controllers, each with the console's own controls. The triggers run 0..255. The sticks run -32768..32767 with up and right positive, as the console has them. Guide is the button in the middle. How many controllers are plugged in is the Controllers setting; the rest are not there, and a game is told so.",
